      "id": 1,
      "text": "Brownsville was founded in 1858 as a settlement of Jewish factory workers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brownsville%2C_Brooklyn",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The neighborhood’s first synagogue, Ohev Sholom, was built inside a factory by Kaplan.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brownsville%2C_Brooklyn",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Stone Avenue branch of the Brooklyn Public Library, once called the Brownsville Children’s Library, was the first library worldwide dedicated solely to children.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://habitatnycwc.org/neighborhood-walking-tour-brownsville/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1913, residents renamed Ames Street to Herzl Street, making it one of the few streets outside Israel bearing that name.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brownsville%2C_Brooklyn",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The area became a historical landmark in 2013 to honor the legacy of slavery and the African diaspora.",
